---
import ArticleClosingNote from '@/components/articles/ArticleClosingNote.astro';
import ArticleImage from '@/components/ui/ArticleImage.astro';
import { articleHref } from '@/domains/articles/catalog';
import nambaYasakaShrine from '@/assets/articles/osaka/osaka-namba-yasaka-shrine.webp';
import springColours from '@/assets/articles/osaka/spring-colours-at-katsuoji-temple.webp';
import katsuojiStampCard from '@/assets/articles/osaka/katsuoji-stamp-card.webp';
import ichiranDiningBooths from '@/assets/articles/osaka/osaka-the-famous-ichiran-dining-booths.webp';
import ichiranRamen from '@/assets/articles/osaka/osaka-lunch-at-ichiran-ramen.webp';
import toichiBrulee from '@/assets/articles/osaka/osaka-toichi-brulee-pancakes.webp';
import toichiStrawberry from '@/assets/articles/osaka/osaka-toichi-strawberry-pancakes.webp';
import dotonboriSunset from '@/assets/articles/osaka/osaka-dotonbori-sunset.webp';
import dotonboriAfterSunset from '@/assets/articles/osaka/osaka-dotonbori-after-sunset.webp';
import tenYenSnack from '@/assets/articles/osaka/osaka-the-famous-10-yen-coin-snack.webp';
import takoyaki from '@/assets/articles/osaka/osaka-takoyaki.webp';
import uniqueStorefronts from '@/assets/articles/osaka/osaka-unique-storefronts.webp';
import ArticleGoogleMap from '@/components/articles/ArticleGoogleMap.astro';

const prose = 'article-prose max-w-3xl text-black/80 [&_p]:mb-5 [&_p:last-child]:mb-0';
const mapLink = 'text-[#69746E] underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-[#4a534f]';
const twoColGrid = 'my-5 grid grid-cols-2 items-start gap-3 md:my-8 md:gap-4';
const mapId = '1DIvaJpYXYGz4VNKzcCK100oeSaBFc6E';

--- 


<ArticleGoogleMap
  title="Google Map of Osaka"
  description="You'll find every stop mentioned in this guide pinned on the interactive map, making it easy to follow the same route during your visit."
  mapId={mapId}
/>
<section id="section-0" class="scroll-mt-nav">
  <div class="mb-5 max-w-3xl text-black md:mb-6">
    <h3>Start Your Day at Namba Yasaka Shrine</h3>
  </div>
  <div class={prose}>
    <p>Namba Yasaka Shrine is best known for its giant lion-shaped stage, which is said to swallow evil spirits and bring good fortune. It's one of Osaka's most unique landmarks and an easy first stop before the city starts to get busy.</p>
    <ArticleImage src={nambaYasakaShrine} alt="The giant lion-shaped stage at Namba Yasaka Shrine in Osaka" caption="The lion-shaped stage at Namba Yasaka Shrine" imgClass="aspect-square object-cover" />
    <p>I arrived shortly after sunrise, and the soft morning light made the shrine even more impressive. If you're planning to take photos, I'd definitely recommend getting there early before the crowds arrive.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="section-1" class="scroll-mt-nav">
  <div class="mb-5 max-w-3xl text-black md:mb-6">
    <h3>Visit Katsuoji Temple</h3>
  </div>
  <div class={prose}>
    <p>Katsuoji Temple is a Buddhist temple in the hills north of Osaka, famous for the thousands of daruma dolls displayed throughout the grounds. Although it's outside the city centre, it's one of the most memorable places I visited in Japan and well worth the journey if you have the time.</p>
    <div class={twoColGrid}>
      <ArticleImage src={springColours} alt="Spring colours surrounding Katsuoji Temple" caption="Spring colours at Katsuoji Temple" figureClass="m-0" imgClass="aspect-[4/5] object-cover" />
      <ArticleImage src={katsuojiStampCard} alt="The Katsuoji Temple stamp card" caption="The Katsuoji stamp card" figureClass="m-0" imgClass="aspect-[4/5] object-cover" />
    </div>
    <p>If you're planning a visit, I've also written a complete <a href={articleHref('katsuoji-temple-from-osaka')} class={mapLink}>Katsuoji Temple Guide</a>, where I share how to get there, the best photo spots and everything I wish I'd known before visiting.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="section-2" class="scroll-mt-nav">
  <div class="mb-5 max-w-3xl text-black md:mb-6">
    <h3>Lunch at Ichiran Ramen</h3>
  </div>
  <div class={prose}>
    <p>Ichiran is one of Japan's most famous ramen chains, known for its individual dining booths and customisable tonkotsu ramen. After visiting Katsuoji Temple and making the journey back into Osaka, it's an easy and satisfying place to stop for lunch. The individual booths give you a quiet space to sit down, customise your ramen and enjoy a proper break before continuing with the rest of the day.</p>
    <div class={twoColGrid}>
      <ArticleImage src={ichiranDiningBooths} alt="The famous individual dining booths at Ichiran Ramen in Osaka" caption="The famous Ichiran dining booths" figureClass="m-0" imgClass="aspect-[4/5] object-cover" />
      <ArticleImage src={ichiranRamen} alt="A bowl of customisable ramen at Ichiran in Osaka" caption="Lunch at Ichiran Ramen" figureClass="m-0" imgClass="aspect-[4/5] object-cover" />
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="section-3" class="scroll-mt-nav">
  <div class="mb-5 max-w-3xl text-black md:mb-6">
    <h3>Treat Yourself to Pancakes at Toichi</h3>
  </div>
  <div class={prose}>
    <p>If you still have room for dessert, I'd definitely recommend stopping by Toichi in Kitahorie. I ordered the strawberry pancakes with chocolate mousse, and they ended up being some of the best pancakes I tried during my trip to Japan. If you're looking for a dessert worth making a small detour for, this is it.</p>
    <div class={twoColGrid}>
      <ArticleImage src={toichiBrulee} alt="Brûlée pancakes at Toichi in Kitahorie, Osaka" caption="Toichi's brûlée pancakes" figureClass="m-0" imgClass="aspect-[4/5] object-cover" />
      <ArticleImage src={toichiStrawberry} alt="Strawberry pancakes with chocolate mousse at Toichi in Kitahorie, Osaka" caption="Toichi's strawberry pancakes" figureClass="m-0" imgClass="aspect-[4/5] object-cover" />
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="section-4" class="scroll-mt-nav">
  <div class="mb-5 max-w-3xl text-black md:mb-6">
    <h3>Spend the Evening in Dotonbori</h3>
  </div>
  <div class={prose}>
    <p>No first visit to Osaka feels complete without spending some time in Dotonbori. Once the sun goes down, the neon lights reflect on the canal, the streets fill with people and the whole area comes to life.</p>
    <div class={twoColGrid}>
      <ArticleImage src={dotonboriSunset} alt="Dotonbori in Osaka at sunset" caption="Dotonbori at sunset" figureClass="m-0" imgClass="aspect-[4/5] object-cover" />
      <ArticleImage src={dotonboriAfterSunset} alt="The neon lights of Dotonbori in Osaka after sunset" caption="Dotonbori after sunset" figureClass="m-0" imgClass="aspect-[4/5] object-cover" />
    </div>
    <p>Most visitors head straight for the famous Glico Runner sign, but don't stop there. Walk across the bridge, explore the surrounding streets and keep an eye out for the giant animated dog billboard, which ended up being one of my favourite discoveries in the area. I lost count of how many times I stopped to take another photo.</p>
    <div class={twoColGrid}>
      <ArticleImage src={tenYenSnack} alt="The famous 10-yen coin-shaped cheese snack in Osaka" caption="The famous 10-yen coin snack" figureClass="m-0" imgClass="aspect-[4/5] object-cover" />
      <ArticleImage src={takoyaki} alt="Freshly made takoyaki in Osaka" caption="Osaka takoyaki" figureClass="m-0" imgClass="aspect-[4/5] object-cover" />
    </div>
    <ArticleImage src={uniqueStorefronts} alt="The unique, creative storefronts of Dotonbori in Osaka" caption="Dotonbori's unique storefronts" imgClass="aspect-[3/2] object-cover" />
  </div>
</section>

<section id="section-5" class="scroll-mt-nav">
  <div class="mb-5 max-w-3xl text-black md:mb-6">
    <h3>FAQ</h3>
  </div>
  <div class={prose}>
    <h4 class="mb-2 text-black">Is Osaka Worth Visiting on Your First Trip to Japan?</h4>
    <p>Yes, Osaka is definitely worth visiting on your first trip to Japan. It has a completely different feel from Tokyo and Kyoto, with incredible food, lively neighbourhoods and easy access to nearby places like Nara.</p>
    <p>Even if you only have one day, Osaka is still worth adding to your itinerary. Between Namba Yasaka Shrine, Dotonbori and Katsuoji Temple, the city gives you a very different experience from the rest of Japan's classic first-time route.</p>
    <h4 class="mt-8 mb-2 text-black">Is One Day Enough for Osaka?</h4>
    <p>Yes, one day is enough to see many of Osaka's main highlights. That said, whether you should stay longer really depends on the kind of trip you're planning.</p>
    <p>If you're not planning to stay in Kyoto, Osaka can be an excellent base instead. Hotels are often more affordable, and it's easy to take day trips to Kyoto, Nara or Universal Studios Japan while staying in the city. If that's your plan, spending a few extra days in Osaka makes perfect sense.</p>
    <h4 class="mt-8 mb-2 text-black">Where Should You Stay in Osaka?</h4>
    <p>If it's your first time visiting Osaka, I'd recommend staying in either Namba or Umeda. Namba puts you within walking distance of Dotonbori, restaurants and many of the city's main attractions, while Umeda is a great choice if you're planning day trips or travelling between Osaka and Kyoto.</p>
    <h4 class="mt-8 mb-2 text-black">How Do You Get Around Osaka?</h4>
    <p>Getting around Osaka is really easy. During my trip, I used a Suica card on trains and the Osaka Metro, while Google Maps made planning every journey simple and surprisingly accurate.</p>
    <p>Many of Osaka's main attractions are also within walking distance of each other, especially around Namba and Dotonbori. Walking ended up being one of my favourite ways to explore the city, as it gave me the chance to discover streets, shops and restaurants I would have otherwise missed. If it's your first time visiting Japan, I'd recommend picking up a Suica card as soon as you arrive—it'll make travelling around Osaka and other cities much more convenient.</p>
    <h4 class="mt-8 mb-2 text-black">How Do You Get to Osaka?</h4>
    <p>Getting to Osaka is really easy. If you're already travelling around Japan, you'll probably be using the country's excellent rail network. Kyoto is less than 30 minutes away by train, while the Shinkansen from Tokyo takes around 2.5 hours.</p>
    <p>If you're flying into Japan, you'll most likely arrive at Kansai International Airport, which is well connected to the city by train and bus. Throughout my trip, I found Google Maps to be the easiest way to plan every journey.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="section-6" class="scroll-mt-nav">
  <div class={prose}>
    <ArticleClosingNote label="Final thoughts">
      <p>If you only have one day in Osaka, don't feel like you have to fit everything in. Some of my favourite memories came from places like Namba Yasaka Shrine and Katsuoji Temple rather than the city's busiest attractions. I hope this itinerary helps you discover a side of Osaka that stays with you long after your trip.</p>
      <p>Before your trip, don't forget to save the interactive map so you can easily follow this itinerary once you're in Osaka. Have an amazing trip, and I hope you create some unforgettable memories of your own.</p>
    </ArticleClosingNote>
  </div>
</section>
